| test | line | failing assertion | received |
|---|---|---|---|
| `refuses to re-point a line while the invoice save is in flight (round 4, F1)` | `purchase.test.tsx:1674` | `purchase-line-new-item-منتج` `.disabled === true` — the line's create trigger must be dead while the save is out | `false` — the control stayed usable, which is precisely the reported defect |
| `refuses a line-create submit that no disabled button produced (round 4, F1 belt)` | `purchase.test.tsx:1712` | `expect(createdItems).toHaveLength(0)` — no create may start behind the in-flight save | length **1** — a product was created against a request already serialised |
| `refuses a catalog pick that would re-point a line under the in-flight save (round 4, F1 belt)` | `purchase.test.tsx:1742` | `expect(purchase-line-pick-item-بديل).toBeNull()` — the line keeps the product the request carries | an `HTMLButtonElement` — the line **was** re-pointed at the picked product while the save was out |
| `freezes the rest of the invoice draft while the save is in flight (round 4, F1)` | `purchase.test.tsx:1767` | `lineQty(...,"item-منتج").disabled === true` | `false` — the quantity the request already carried stayed editable |

Rows two and three are the ordering proof in its rawest form: with the save in
flight, a submit and a pick both changed the line after `submit()` had already
mapped it into the request body. Row two started a real `itemsCreate` behind the
request; row three re-pointed the line at `بديل` while the payload still named
`item-منتج`.

The failing React assertion dumps the whole DOM node (~8.5M lines, tens of MB),
so the raw tail is deliberately not kept: the verdict is the exit code and the
named assertion is in the head.

